{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"gummous-illadvised-stingray","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Preemptive hardening of low-headroom, high-centrality modules\n\n## Context / Problem\nADR 0056 relieved `runner.py` — the single highest-churn file in the repo (27 commits per 400) —\nfrom 689 to 647 LOC, giving it 153 lines of headroom. The treatment worked. It was never applied to\nthe next tier, and that tier is now blocking work.\n\nMEASURED (churn per 400 commits / fan-in / LOC / headroom against the 800 cap in\n`.github/module-size-limit.txt`):\n\n  llm/config.py                 16 / 93 / 793 /  7   <- most central near-cap file in the repo\n  llm/plan_review/passes.py     13 /  - / 800 /  0\n  signing.py                     - / 42 / 798 /  2\n  llm/plan_review/attest.py     21 / 38 / 800 /  0   (ticket c6c9)\n  llm/plan_review/workflow_ops  16 /  - / 794 /  6\n  _commands/transition_close    23 /  - / 793 /  7\n  llm/plan_review/__init__.py   24 /  - / 768 / 32\n  llm/runner.py                 27 / 68 / 647 / 153  <- ADR 0056; the proof the treatment works\n\nSIX files sit within 7 lines of the cap. Three are at EXACTLY 800. This is already blocking:\nticket d23e's entire scope is `llm/config.py`, and a deprecation alias there costs 10-16 lines\nagainst 7 available. MEASURED cost of one new config knob is 10-13 lines (`9ad46965bd` 34 lines/3\nknobs, `1f63d74a03` 21/2, `75d1a4672d` 22/2, `434dc0a577` 26/2), so the headroom is less than ONE\nknob.\n\n## Approach\nRelieve these files SEPARATELY and AHEAD of the work that must touch them, so no ticket discovers\nthe breach mid-flight the way d23e just did.\n\n**The method is ADR 0056's, generalized: diagnose the SHAPE of the mass, then pick the matching\nmove.** The ADR proves this by declining to generalize ITSELF onto config.py (§Decision 5). Three\nshapes observed:\n * ABSORBING VERB — one long method every feature edits. Move: extract verbs as plain functions with\n   explicit parameters. (`runner.run()`, 59% of its file.)\n * FLAT DECLARATION TABLE — N near-identical per-field reads. Move: data-driven table.\n * COLOCATED DOMAINS — independent units merely sharing a file. Move: split on the domain seam.\n\n**The test of any split, from the ADR: does it remove the thing that ABSORBS FUTURE CHANGE, or\nmerely relocate mass?** Extracting a callee always satisfies a per-FILE gate while the calling method\nkeeps absorbing — which is why runner.py regrew three times. Where a split only relocates mass, SAY\nSO in the change rather than claiming permanence.\n\n## The per-function ratchet is REJECTED — do not re-propose it\nADR 0056 §Decision 1 deferred a per-FUNCTION length gate and said \"if the pattern recurs a fourth\ntime, the per-function gate is the answer\". It was raised and the operator DECLINED it: blast radius\ntoo high. MEASURED justification, from the ADR's own figures — 3,292 functions under `src/rebar`, 21\nover 200 lines, 38 over 150, 62 over 120, 101 over 100, with the ten worst spanning six subsystems.\nIt would break six subsystems at once or require an allowlist, and epic 716f deliberately DRAINED\nAND REMOVED the module-size allowlist.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, and the reason this epic matters: the ratchet was the named remedy for ADR 0056's stated\nresidual risk (\"nothing structurally prevents the decomposed run() from regrowing\"). With it\ndeclined, module-level rework plus SEAM TESTS are the only defence. The seam test is therefore\nload-bearing, not decorative: change 1484 added `< 700` headroom assertions for `gate_dispatch.py`\nand `orchestrator.py`, and those two are the comfortable files today BECAUSE of it. Every child here\nships a headroom guard for the file it relieves.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` -> a new `src/rebar/llm/gate_context.py` (child: unblocks d23e)\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/workflow_ops.py` -> a new `plan_review/decide_ops.py`\n- `scripts/gen_env_registry.py` (a found defect: `_llm_float` is invisible to the scanner)\n- `tests/unit/` seam guards, including the two static checks that exist at NO layer today\n- NOT in scope: `passes.py`, `attest.py` (c6c9), `signing.py`, `transition_close.py`. Recorded as\n  known-remaining so the next reader is not misled into thinking this epic drains the tier.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `llm/config.py` is at or below 640 LOC, and d23e can land its deprecation alias without any\n      structural change of its own\n- [ ] `llm/plan_review/workflow_ops.py` is at or below 560 LOC\n- [ ] a seam test asserts a headroom target for EACH relieved file, and every new module clears the\n      100-LOC anti-fragmentation floor AGENTS.md requires\n- [ ] a test asserts the full expected plan-review step-registry membership — the guard that does not\n      exist at any layer today, so a missed registration import currently cannot be caught statically\n- [ ] a test asserts every `uses:` in `gates/plan-review.yaml` resolves in `STEP_REGISTRY`\n- [ ] `python scripts/gen_env_registry.py --check` is clean AND complete, i.e. no env var read\n      through a helper is missing from `docs/env-vars.md`\n- [ ] every relocation lands with ZERO edits to existing tests; a required test edit means the cut is\n      wrong and is the revert signal","id":"9227-218b-03e8-41bd","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Preemptive hardening of low-headroom, high-centrality modules"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785561923473917001,"uuid":"47b3419c-04df-4dd9-b27d-c0ae6fd2bc24"}